# CI workflow security (GitHub Actions & GitLab CI)

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic security rules to the risks
specific to workflow-YAML configuration. Applies only to GitHub Actions
workflow files under `.github/workflows/`, GitHub composite `action.yml`/
`action.yaml` files under `.github/actions/`, `.gitlab-ci.yml`, and GitLab
CI included config under `.gitlab/`.

## `pull_request_target` and untrusted fork code

- `pull_request_target` runs in the context of the **base** repository —
  every repo secret the workflow references is available to it (secrets
  are consumed, not read/write-scoped, so "access" is simply yes/no here),
  and `GITHUB_TOKEN` gets read/write repository permissions by default
  (unless a `permissions:` block explicitly narrows it) — even though the
  triggering pull request can come from an untrusted fork.
- Never check out and then execute the pull request's own head ref/SHA
  (`actions/checkout` with `ref: ${{ github.event.pull_request.head.sha }}`)
  inside a `pull_request_target` job and then run a build/test/lint
  command against it — that combination (checkout untrusted code + run it
  with base-repo secrets) is the "pwn request" pattern: it hands an
  attacker-controlled pull request everything it needs to exfiltrate
  secrets or push with the workflow's own write access.
- If a `pull_request_target` job genuinely needs to read fork content
  (e.g. to comment on a PR), check it out read-only and do not execute
  anything from it — no `run:`, no build step, no test step against the
  fork's own code — or route the job through a privilege-separated
  `workflow_run` split (a build job with no secrets, a separate
  privileged job that only consumes safe artifacts) instead.
- When a job only needs to build/test a contribution and does not need
  base-repo secrets or write access, use `pull_request`, not
  `pull_request_target` — when a `pull_request` run is triggered from a
  fork, `GITHUB_TOKEN` (still the base repo's own token, not a separate
  "fork token") gets read-only permissions, and no other repo secret is
  passed to the runner at all.

## Pin third-party actions to a commit SHA

- Reference a third-party action by its full-length commit SHA
  (`uses: actions/checkout@<40-char-sha>`), not a mutable tag like `@v4`
  or a branch name — a tag can be moved by the action's own maintainer
  (or, if the account is compromised, by an attacker) to point at a
  different, malicious commit without the reference in your workflow ever
  changing.
- A first-party action from `actions/*` maintained by GitHub itself is
  lower risk than an arbitrary third-party action, but the SHA-pinning
  rule still applies to any action outside the repository's own
  `.github/actions/` — do not special-case a well-known org as exempt.
- When a comment noting the tag the SHA corresponds to helps readability
  (`uses: actions/checkout@<sha> # v4.2.0`), that comment is documentation
  only — it is the SHA, not the comment, that pins the actual code that
  runs.

## Least-privilege `permissions:`

- Default to `permissions: {}` (or `permissions: read-all` only when read
  access is genuinely needed everywhere) at the workflow level, then grant
  the specific scopes a job needs (`contents: write`, `pull-requests:
  write`, `id-token: write` for OIDC, etc.) at the **job** level, not the
  workflow level — a workflow-level `permissions: write-all` (or no
  `permissions:` key at all, which can default to broad access depending
  on the repository/org setting) hands every job in the file whatever
  scope the most-privileged job needed, even ones that only read.
- Grant `id-token: write` only to the job that actually performs OIDC
  federation (cloud credential exchange); never at the workflow level
  alongside jobs that do not need it.

## Script injection via untrusted expression expansion

- Never interpolate an untrusted `${{ github.event.* }}` value (a PR
  title, issue body, commit message, branch name, or any other
  attacker-influenced field) directly into a `run:` shell string — the
  expression is substituted into the generated script **before** the
  shell runs, so a value containing shell metacharacters (`"`, `` ` ``,
  `$(...)`) can break out of the intended string and execute arbitrary
  commands on the runner with whatever permissions that job has.
- Pass the untrusted value through an intermediate `env:` variable first,
  then reference the environment variable (`"$TITLE"`, quoted) inside the
  script, instead of interpolating `${{ github.event.pull_request.title
  }}` directly into the `run:` string — the environment variable is set
  in memory and never re-enters shell-script generation, which is what
  actually neutralizes the injection.
- GitLab CI's own script-injection risk is NOT the same mechanism, and the
  `env:`-indirection fix above does not carry over: every GitLab CI/CD
  variable (a predefined one like `CI_MERGE_REQUEST_TITLE`, or one you
  declare under `variables:`) is already an ordinary shell environment
  variable by the time `script:` runs — there is no separate "before the
  shell sees it" substitution step to intercept the way `${{ }}` needs
  routing through `env:` first. Wrapping an already-a-variable in another
  `variables:` entry changes nothing about how the shell expands it.
  The real risks, and their real fixes:
  - **Unquoted expansion**: `script: - rm -rf $CI_MERGE_REQUEST_TITLE` lets
    the shell word-split and glob-expand an untrusted value; always quote
    (`"$CI_MERGE_REQUEST_TITLE"`), and never build a command string by
    concatenating an untrusted variable into one for `eval`/`sh -c`
    (`eval "some-command $CI_MERGE_REQUEST_TITLE"` is exploitable however
    the variable got its value).
  - **Config-time interpolation via CI/CD inputs** (`$[[ inputs.x ]]`,
    used in a component/included config's `rules:`/`script:`): this IS
    substituted into the YAML/expression text before the job is even
    created, the same class of risk `${{ }}` is in GitHub Actions — do not
    feed an untrusted pipeline or trigger input value (a `spec:inputs`
    value the pipeline/component was invoked with, not a predefined
    variable like an MR title) into an `inputs:`-driven `$[[ ]]`
    interpolation that reaches a `script:` line or a `rules:if:`
    expression without validating/escaping it first.

## GitLab CI protected variables and branches

- A CI/CD variable marked "Protected" is only exposed to pipelines running
  on a protected branch or a protected tag — an unprotected branch's
  pipeline (including one from a fork merge request, when forks are
  allowed) never receives it. Do not rely on a protected variable being
  present in a job whose `rules:`/`only:`/`except:` allow it to run on a
  non-protected ref; the value will simply be empty there, not an error.
- Mark any variable carrying a credential, deploy token, or signing
  secret both "Protected" and "Masked" and restrict the job that consumes
  it to protected branches/tags via `rules:` — a deploy job reachable from
  an unprotected branch or an open merge request should not have access
  to production credentials at all.

## Anti-patterns to flag

- A `pull_request_target` job that checks out `github.event.pull_request.head.sha`
  (or `.ref`) and then runs a build/test/lint step against that checkout.
- Any `uses:` line pinned to a tag (`@v4`, `@main`) instead of a full
  commit SHA for a third-party action.
- A workflow-level `permissions: write-all`, or no `permissions:` block at
  all, on a workflow with jobs that do not all need write access.
- `${{ github.event.* }}` interpolated directly inside a `run:` string
  instead of passed through `env:` first.
- A GitLab CI `script:` unquoted use of a variable holding untrusted input
  (an MR title, a branch name), or that variable concatenated into a
  string handed to `eval`/`sh -c`.
- An untrusted value reaching a GitLab CI/CD `inputs:`-driven `$[[ ]]`
  interpolation in a `script:` line or a `rules:if:` expression with no
  validation.
